{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1791383477,"nonce":"wLowFHxY3x4h0+0/YBeS4lHFIJo=","title":"SSA: bound and reuse inherited variable reads during final phi filling","message":"Cranelift-only startup profiles at 76bc7ca9bb0884e41b4b835e0cfe65dc3bfb707d: NUC shared lower_program takes 55.29 ms for Python FFT, 42.61 ms for chaos, 27.75 ms for Lua nbody and 17.53 ms for method_call. Lua method_call also spends 44 ms optimizing its main chunk before 8.49 ms of translation/native compilation; that is separate follow-up work.\n\nA five-second local ARM64 stack sample of ZYPY_PROFILE_STARTUP=1000 on staged FFT identifies fill_incomplete_phis/read_variable as 511 of 3785 samples on the startup worker. Repeated predecessor queries reconstruct the same inherited variable values. The current code intentionally avoids caching them in definitions because a block-by-variable matrix caused high transient memory use. Caching while desugaring still changes edges would also be unsafe.\n\nExperiment: memoize at most 4096 (block, variable) inherited reads only during final phi filling, after all source assignments and CFG edges are final. Explicit definitions take precedence; reads still resolve pending phi substitutions. Release the cache as soon as filling ends. Preserve the uncached construction path and expose a diagnostic disable flag. Test loop/merge substitutions, cache bounds, conformance and generated output; compare startup, short/long walls, and RSS on the NUC with LLVM disabled.\n\nProfiles: /tmp/zyntax-startup-{lua,python}-*.trace and /tmp/zyntax-startup-sample.txt locally; phase traces also on the NUC. NUC perf is unavailable without an interactive sudo login, so stack sampling used the local release process and phase attribution used the NUC. No threshold or runtime dispatch change is proposed.","files":null}]}